---
title: Data & remote functions
summary: All six Kit remote primitives operate inside islands: query, command, form, and more.
related:
  - regions/held-regions
  - app/spa-router
---

Islands are real Svelte components (see the [last chapter](/docs/regions/regions)). Thus the remote functions of Kit operate inside them, without changes. All six primitives (`query`, `query.batch`, `query.live`, `command`, `form`, and `prerender`) run in an island exactly as they do in a Kit page.

::: tip When to use
Remote functions are how an island speaks to the server: read data (`query`), change it (`command` / `form`), or stream it (`query.live`). Use them when an island needs server data, or must keep a change. They replace the fetch-and-endpoint boilerplate that you would write, and the server validates each call.
:::

```svelte
<script>
  import { greeting } from './hello.remote';
  const who = await greeting();
</script>

<p>{who}</p>
```

SSR query results are seeded into the client cache. Thus an island that reads a query during the server render does not fetch again on hydrate, in production. Dev degrades to a refetch. See the note below.

## Try it

This counter is a client island. It reads a `query` and mutates through two `command`s. The value lives on the server. Each button is a real round-trip. A `.refresh()` then reads the value again. It is shared state: open this page in a second tab, and both tabs move.

<script>
  import CounterDemo from './CounterDemo.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo">
  <CounterDemo />
</div>

```ts title=counter.remote.ts
let count = 3;
export const getCount = query(async () => count);
export const bump = command(async () => (count += 1));
```

## Forms and commands

`form()` and `command()` POST to their endpoints, and operate from inside an island. On success, `form()` runs `invalidateAll` from Kit. ogygia treats that as a **soft seed refresh**: it fetches the current page again, merges `<head>`, and refreshes the in-place data seeds. There is no view transition, no body swap, no island remount, and no clear of live queries.

```svelte
<script>
  import { addEntry } from './guestbook.remote';
</script>

<form {...addEntry}>
  <input name="msg" />
  <button>Sign</button>
</form>
```

If an island needs a query to update after a mutation, call `.refresh()` on it. Or pair `submit().updates(query)` with server-side `requested(query).refreshAll()`. `updates` alone does not fill the response.

## Mutation guards

A write to a captured host variable from island markup is a mistake. The value is a serialized snapshot, not live state. In dev, ogygia wraps each snapshot in a **deep proxy**. The proxy does a `console.warn`, with the path of the prop, when you mutate one. The write still applies. It is only a warning. Treat it as the signal to move that state into the island.

::: warning Dev is not prod
In dev, SSR query seeding degrades to a refetch, and the mutation proxy runs only in dev. Check data flows against a production build.
:::

## Do / don't

- **Do** call `.refresh()` (or `submit().updates(q)` plus server `requested(q).refreshAll()`) when a mutation must update a query. `updates` alone does not fill the response.
- **Do** test data flows against a production build. Dev seeding and the mutation guard behave differently.
- **Don't** mutate a captured host prop from island markup. It is a serialized snapshot. Move mutable state inside the island.

